\f0\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 In my previous emails, I've shared why conventional treatments often fail and how the Amari Method takes a fundamentally different approach.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs26 Today, I want to give you something practical you can try right now.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs26 This is our signature Power Posture technique \'96 a simple but powerful exercise that helps keep your shoulders and shoulder blades in proper alignment and retrains your upper body to stay upright instead of slumping forward.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs26 This will relieve tension in your shoulders, shoulder blades, and upper back...and will also help relieve tension in the neck.
\f1\fs24 \

\f3\b\fs26 While standing:
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Interlace your fingers behind your back & squeeze your shoulder blades together as if you're squeezing a pencil between them
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Keep your elbows slightly-to-moderately bent. 
\f2\i Your elbows should not be straight.
\f0\i0  We want the movement to occur between the shoulder blades, not in the elbows.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Focus on the sound of your breath. As you exhale, you'll be able to squeeze & engage your shoulder blades more & more. Keep your shoulder blades squeezed the entire time, but not so hard that it cuts off your breathing. You want the perfect balance between keeping your shoulder blades squeezing together the whole time - while allowing the breath to help you more fully engage the shoulder blades as you exhale. This will help you balance out all of the muscles in your upper back.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 Remember, the reason we have tension, pain and dysfunction is because some muscles are working too hard BECAUSE other muscles aren't working enough. This is about symmetry & balance.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do this twice a day, every day
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The goal is to work up to being able to hold this for 90 seconds, or more.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 At first this may be difficult because most of the muscles in our back are not used to being properly aligned. This retraining of our shoulder blades (and the muscles between them) will keep our shoulder blades flat against our back. This will help counter the misalignment that comes from sitting too much & many other things we do that misalign & strain our shoulders & upper back.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Keep your knees soft while doing this. We don't want our knees or our elbows to be locked.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Small bouncing movements on the balls of your feet will help keep your shoulder blades moving towards each other on the exhale.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Focus on the sound of your exhale and your entire body relaxing. The only thing that should be fully engaging the entire time is your shoulder blades. Try to notice if any other parts of the body are working too hard.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs26 \cf0 \strokec2 This can be very challenging at first. It will get easier every time you do it \'97 and you'll be able to hold it for longer if you'd like. The rewards of doing this daily are outstanding & quite noticeable.
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs26 I'd love to hear how this works for you. Many of my clients report feeling a profound sense of relief even the first time they try it.
